  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Anticipate cooler temperatures and periods of valley rain and \nmountain with the weather pattern through the week.\n\nThe first low pressure system is currently over the Northern\nRockies causing wrap around precipitation to stream into northwest\nMontana this morning. Snow levels appear to be hovering around\n5000 feet so Marias pass could be experiencing some snow\naccumulations. The remainder of the region will experience\nscattered showers throughout the day as temperatures will be\nrunning around 5 degrees below seasonal averages. \n\nA secondary weaker disturbance is still expected to move across\nthe Northern Rockies later tonight into Wednesday morning\nenhancing the shower activity especially across north central\nIdaho into northwest Montana.\n\nA stronger low pressure system will develop just off the Pacific\nNorthwest coast by Thursday morning, drawing slightly cooler air\nto be pulled over the Continental Divide around Glacier National\nPark. This cooler air will cause snow levels to drop to around\n3000 feet and any linger shower activity Wednesday night into\nThursday morning could cause some snow accumulations down to West\nGlacier and Hungry Horse area. \n\nThis low will shift slightly to the east Friday into Saturday\ncausing southwesterly upper level flow to develop over the\nNorthern Rockies. Temperatures will warm to more seasonal readings\nas snow levels rise back to around 5000 feet. \n\nThe latest long term models are starting to indicate that the low\nwill move into the region by Sunday into Monday bringing enhanced\nprecipitation.",
